      "title": "hook (12 light-years or 7.2?)",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "Here's where we stopped. In Earth's frame, the star is twelve light years away, and the trip at zero point eight c takes fifteen years. The ship's clock reads nine years, and in the ship's frame the star rushes towards it at zero point eight c. So for the ship, the distance is only seven point two light years, shorter by exactly gamma, one point six seven. So is the space between Earth and the star being squashed? Make your prediction. Nothing is pushed or squashed at all. So why can a moving distance be shorter without an object being crushed?"
      },
