    {
      "id": "contraction-proper-length",
      "title": "length contraction and proper length",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "This is length contraction. A length measured in a frame where the object is moving is shorter than its length at rest, by the factor gamma. On your data sheet, l equals l zero times the square root of one minus v squared over c squared, which is the same as l zero over gamma. l zero is the proper length, the length in the frame where the object is at rest. Earth and the star are at rest in Earth's frame, so twelve light years is the proper length. Proper length is always the longest."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 0,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "derivation",
      "title": "where it comes from",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "Where does it come from? Time a spacecraft as it passes a marker beacon on a space station. In the station's frame, the nose passes the beacon, then the tail passes, both at the same place, so the beacon's clock measures proper time, t zero. The station's length for the ship is v times t zero. In the ship's frame, the beacon slides along the hull, from nose to tail, taking the longer time, gamma t zero. So the ship's own length is v times gamma t zero. Divide them, and the station measures l zero over gamma. Length contraction comes straight out of time dilation."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 0,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "measure-moving-length",
      "title": "measuring a moving length",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "There's a catch in measuring anything that moves. You have to mark both ends at the same time, in your frame. Mark the nose, wait, then mark the tail, and the ship has moved, so your answer is wrong. And here's the key. Observers in different frames don't agree on which distant events happen at the same time. So they don't agree on which pair of end marks gives the length. That's why each frame gets a different, correct answer."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 0,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "not-crushed",
      "title": "nothing is crushed, yet it's real",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "Now the crushing question. Nothing pushes on the ship. No force acts, and no atoms are squeezed. On board, every ruler gives the full length, l zero, as always. And it's reciprocal. The ship measures the station's rulers as shorter too. They can't both be squashed. That doesn't make it fake. In the station's frame, the ship really is shorter. Length depends on the frame, just as time does, and in no frame does anything feel squeezed."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 0,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
    {
      "id": "perpendicular",
      "title": "only along the motion",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "Contraction only happens along the direction of motion. Lengths at right angles don't change. Here's why. Put identical rings on two spacecraft flying straight at each other. If sideways lengths shrank, each ship would say the other's ring is smaller and passes inside its own. They can't both be right, so sideways lengths can't change. That's why the light clock's mirrors stayed d apart."
      },
      "durationInFrames": 1,
      "holdSeconds": 0,
      "holdKind": "visual"
    },
